#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221097 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "41b1a655-6092-57b6-8f95-396c566e7a83" :question/math-academy-id "e-21445" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\na \\ge 0\n$$\n\nand $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\}, \\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-2s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+2)\\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ng(t)=t^2+3t+2\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(t + 2) &= (t + 2)^{2} + 3(t + 2) + 2 \\\\\n&= t^{2} + 4t + 4 + 3t + 6 + 2 \\\\\n&= t^{2} + 7t + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s}\n$$\n\nfor $s>0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t + 2)} &= L{t^{2} + 7t + 12} \\\\\n&= L{t^{2}} + 7L{t} + 12L{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s^{3}} + 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s^{2}} + 12 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{7}{s^{2}} + \\frac{12}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{u(t - 2)g(t)} &= e^{-2s}L{g(t + 2)} \\\\\n&= e^{-2s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{7}{s^{2}} + \\frac{12}{s})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221107 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "88ee94b3-1f6e-587a-a63f-736f9df7186e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21582" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}L{\\sin ωt}|= \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0, \\\\ L{\\cos ωt}|= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t-4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\na \\ge 0\n$$\n\nand $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\}, \\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t -4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-4s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+4)\\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ng(t)= \\cos(9\\pi t)\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(t + 4) &= \\cos(9π(t + 4)) \\\\\n&= \\cos (9πt + 36π) \\\\\n&= \\cos 9πt\\cos 36π - \\sin 9πt\\sin 36π \\\\\n&= \\cos 9πt \\cdot 1 - \\sin 9πt \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= \\cos 9πt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\right\\}=\\dfrac{s}{s^2 + \\omega^2}\n$$\n\nfor $s>0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t + 4)} &= L{\\cos (9πt)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + 81π^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{u(t - 4)g(t)} &= e^{-4s}L{g(t + 4)} \\\\\n&= e^{-4s} \\cdot \\frac{s}{s^{2} + 81π^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221146 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Form of the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "1b27851d-3630-506e-972c-b6cef8dbeae3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21864" :question/problem "Find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{u(t-1)(t-1)^3 \\right\\}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\na \\ge 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n$u(t-1)(t-1)^3$.\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a = 1$.\n- The shifted function is $f(t-1) = (t-1)^3$.\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-1)$ with $t{:}$\n\n$$\nf(t) = t^3\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function using the rule\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^3\\} = \\dfrac{3!}{s^{3+1}} = \\dfrac{6}{s^4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s)\n$$\n\nby multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor $e^{-s}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{u(t - 1)(t - 1)^{3}} &= e^{-s} \\cdot F(s) \\\\\n&= e^{-s} \\cdot \\frac{6}{s^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a82bf537-e6b4-513e-a4c8-69fc6a878c7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{5s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} - \\frac{14}{s^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220170} {:answer/id #uuid "aba06933-95e7-5855-8767-609ef63cad94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-5s}(\\frac{1}{s^{3}} + \\frac{4}{s^{2}} + \\frac{1}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220171} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d8cad3-8704-5612-8e63-bc6c02fe05ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}} + \\frac{46}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220172} {:answer/id #uuid "1cea79c8-83c1-59f7-987d-5cf603fb40ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}} + \\frac{46}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220173} {:answer/id #uuid "68da82fc-f009-549f-81eb-94158587bd2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cea79c8-83c1-59f7-987d-5cf603fb40ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}} + \\frac{46}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220173} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcb63e8a-68c5-5d42-9a99-90049bceeae1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220169}] :question/id #uuid "067ef9de-f25f-5573-a8ab-acd02fde3334" :question/math-academy-id "q-331016" :question/problem "Given that $L{t^{n}} = \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}$ for $s > 0$, find $L{u(t - 5)(t^{2} + 4t + 1)}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Form of the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4420a37-bef7-5a94-b616-62d8b9551ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-3s} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s - 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220809} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d8c430-7310-5bdc-8a68-a73311588ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-3s} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s + 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220810} {:answer/id #uuid "f002416c-3c62-555f-9c34-2e7